WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:01.790
- The world looks to China

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:03.980
for signs of future economic growth

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.460
but its economy has seen a sharp slowdown.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.730
In the third quarter,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:12.160
China's economy grew 4.9%
compared to a year earlier,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.090
slowing down from the previous
quarter's 7.9% growth rate.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.990
It's another piece of bad
news after Evergrande.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.480
China's most indebted property developer

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.710
has kept global markets on edge.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.180
So what do China's woes
mean for the global economy?

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.270
The Chinese government is dealing

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.180
with a combination of headwinds.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.250
Policymakers paired back
stimulus measures put in place

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.650
during the pandemic last year.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.960
This came around the same time

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.090
as a regulatory crackdown
on the technology,

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.800
private education, and
real estate sectors.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.950
This worried investors.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.134
Another worry, concerns about Evergrande

00:00:47.134 --> 00:00:49.040
whose troubles have rattled markets

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.810
and raised questions about
China's overall economic

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:52.870
and financial health.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.130
Home sales and construction of new houses

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.370
both fell sharply in September.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.660
There's also a growing energy crisis,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.334
driven by soaring coal prices,

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:03.790
and efforts to lower energy use,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.230
as well as disruptions to supply chains.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.010
The question is now whether these risks

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.170
to the economy will crimp China's growth

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.990
in the final months of the year.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:13.080
Beijing has made it clear

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.080
that it wants to take
on long-festering issues

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.330
in the economy,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.940
such as reining in household
and corporate debt,

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.860
and pursuing ambitious climate goals.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.150
In 2020, China emerged as
the only major world economy

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.960
to report GDP growth amid the pandemic.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.570
The government is
targeting annual GDP growth

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.860
of 6% or more this year.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.670
Most economists are still confident

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.660
that China will be able to achieve this.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.627
Some have begun to lower their forecasts.

00:01:38.627 --> 00:01:41.294
(pensive music)

